---
// Method sheet — Ducking (2026-11-01). The six parts of the method-sheet duty (werkgruppe spec §3.5).
import MethodSheet from '@/components/experiments/MethodSheet.astro'
import { readDesk } from '@/lib/ducking/desk-data'
import { FLOOR_DB, fmtCount, fmtShare, WINDOW_DAYS } from '@/lib/ducking/mix'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de'
const link = 'underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-fg'
const { round, ledger, sources, whole } = readDesk()
---

<MethodSheet title="Method sheet — Ducking" workHref="/ducking" workLabel="To the work">
  <section class="sheet-reveal mb-10">
    <h2 class="kicker mb-2">What this is</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      A share of attention, set as a mix. The matters of one of the ecology’s briefings and everything else on Common Ground’s
      charts are channels on one desk; each channel’s level is its share of the trailing week, and each is a voice. The claim is
      checkable from the committed files: how little of what the charts held named any of these matters. The desk adds no finding
      to that count. It makes the count something you have to hold up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">1. Sources &amp; licences</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        The briefing — <a class={link} href={round.briefing.url}>{round.briefing.title}</a>, written {round.briefing.written}, pinned
        to its commit. Its matters, its order, and the figures the voices read, each with the page it comes from.
      </li>
      <li>
        Common Ground’s day files, {ledger.days[0]} to {ledger.days.at(-1)} — open data (CC0) in
        <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/trending`}>src/data/trending</a>, {sources} sources on the newest day.
      </li>
      <li>
        The voices — Kokoro-82M, open weights under Apache 2.0, run locally on one machine, at no cost and through no service.
        The recordings are CC BY 4.0 with the rest of the lab’s works.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">2. Cadence</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The faders move with Common Ground’s morning ledger: every nightly build counts the new day, and the page shows the week
      to the newest committed day. The voices do not. A round is rendered once, by hand, by
      <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/blob/main/scripts/ducking/voices.py`}>scripts/ducking/voices.py</a>, so its readings stay at
      the briefing’s date until the next round says otherwise, and the page gives that date.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">3. Processing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Count.</b> A signal is one entry on one chart on one day. It names a matter when its label or a link title
        contains one of the matter’s terms as a whole word, case and accents folded, by the rule of
        <a class={link} href="/werke/third-reading">Third Reading</a>. A signal naming two matters counts half for each, so the
        channels and everything else always add up to the day’s signals. This differs from the briefing’s own count, which counted
        raw occurrences in the files; a signal here is a chart entry, not a mention.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Share.</b> A channel’s signals over all signals in the trailing {WINDOW_DAYS} days. The first days of the
        record have shorter windows. Everything else is the remainder, so the sum is the whole ledger.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Level.</b> The share as a power ratio: ten times its logarithm, in decibels. The fader’s travel runs from
        0 dB, the whole, to −{Math.abs(FLOOR_DB)} dB, and each printed mark is ten times the one below. A channel at zero rests on the floor. Each
        channel’s amplitude is the square root of its share, and every recording is levelled to the same speech loudness, so a
        fader’s position means the same on every channel.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Holding.</b> A held channel takes the share its fader says. Everything not held takes what is left, in its
        measured proportions; a channel at zero gets nothing from the room that opens. Hands asking for more than the whole are
        scaled down to it. Holding changes the sound for the person holding, and no count. Nothing a visitor does is sent or kept.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">Everything else.</b> Several voices read a seeded random sample of the labels of the week the round ends,
        each label once, leaving out whatever names a matter, and leaving out a stop list: sexual terms, violence, deaths and named
        crimes, because a machine reading those aloud out of context would be a statement the ledger does not make. The stop list is
        in the script. The sample is drawn once per round, and it loops.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b class="text-fg">The room.</b> One thing is added to the voices: a small synthetic room, in which a quiet channel stands further
        away than a loud one. It changes distance, not level. There is no other sound.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">4. Limits of the method</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>The denominator is the tops of {sources} public charts and feeds: encyclopaedia pages, search trends, app stores, code hosts, social and news sources, each with its own fixed length. It leans to technology and to English, and a longer chart weighs more. Its shares are shares of those charts, not of the world’s attention, however the desk feels.</li>
      <li>A chart measures what rose, was clicked or was posted, by whoever those platforms reach. It says nothing about what anyone thinks or cares about, and nothing about the people a matter happens to.</li>
      <li>A signal is counted the same whatever its size: the top of an encyclopaedia’s list and the bottom of a feed weigh one each.</li>
      <li>The terms are a choice, wide on purpose, in English only. They let in false friends, which the receipts on the work’s page show, and they miss a matter discussed in other words or other languages. The receipts cannot show what was missed.</li>
      <li>A matter is named by its symptoms: Iran’s channel also counts oil and cost-of-living terms, because the briefing frames the matter as an energy shock. The AI-in-the-vote channel does not count the elections themselves, only synthetic media and political ads, so most of the attention on those elections sits in everything else.</li>
      <li>The list of matters is one briefing’s, written by one of the ecology’s machines from the sources it links. It is not a ranking and the desk does not treat it as one.</li>
      <li>The readings are short and they stop at the briefing’s date. Most figures in them are themselves estimates, and some reach the briefing second-hand; each source is linked.</li>
      <li>A desk invites the idea that the mix could be set otherwise by someone at a desk. Nobody sets this one. The metaphor is the work’s, not a finding.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">5. Compute footprint</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      The count runs inside the site’s build, over {fmtCount(whole.total)} signals to date, and costs no service. The voices were
      rendered in a few minutes on one desktop machine, once per round. All audio plays in the visitor’s browser, and loads only
      after the visitor asks for sound.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">6. Change log</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>2026-11-01 — first version, round {round.id}. Over the record at launch the {round.channels.length} matters hold {fmtShare(whole.share)}, a figure this line will not update: the page does.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</MethodSheet>
